===Etymology 1===
{{wikipedia|Dental floss}}
[[Image:Spinning candy floss.jpg|thumb|right|A woman spinning candy floss.]]

{{unc|en}}. Perhaps from {{inh|en|enm|*flos}} (attested in {{cog|enm|Flosmonger|pos=a surname}}), related to {{cog|en|fleece}}. Alternatively from {{bor|en|fr|floche||tuft of wool}}, from {{m|fr|floc}}, from {{der|en|fro|flosche|t=down, velvet}}, from {{der|en|la|floccus|t=piece of wool}}, probably from {{der|en|frk|*flokkō|t=down, wool, flock}}, from {{der|en|gem-pro|*flukkô|t=down, piece of wool, flock}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*plewk-|t=hair, fibres, tuft}}.

Cognate with {{cog|goh|flocko||down}}, {{cog|dum|vlocke||flock}}, {{cog|no|-}} dialectal {{m|no|flugsa||snowflake}}, {{cog|nl|flos||plush}} (tr=17c.).

====Noun====
{{en-noun|~}}

# A [[thread]] used to clean the gaps between the [[tooth|teeth]].
# Raw [[silk]] fibres.
# The fibres covering a [[corncob]] etc.; the loose downy or silky material inside the husks of certain plants, such as beans.
# Any thread-like material having parallel [[strands]] that are not [[spun]] or [[wound]] around each other.
#: {{ux|en|embroidery '''floss'''}}
# {{lb|en|British}} Spun sugar or [[cotton candy]], especially in the phrase "candy floss".
# A [[body]] [[feather]] of an [[ostrich]].
#: {{ux|en|'''Flosses''' are soft, and grey from the female and black from the male.}}
# A [[dance]] [[move]] in which the dancer repeatedly swings their arms, with clenched fists, from the back of their body to the front, on each side.

